Universal Compaction Style is a compaction style, targeting the use cases requiring lower write amplification, trading off read amplification and space amplification.

As introduced by multiple authors and systems, there are two main types of LSM-tree compaction strategies:

- leveled compaction, as the default compaction style in RocksDB
- an alternative compaction strategy, sometimes called "size tiered" [1] or "tiered" [2].


The key difference between the two strategies is that leveled compaction tends to aggressively merge a smaller sorted run into a larger one, while "tiered" waits for several sorted runs with similar size and merge them together.





# First trick Trivial Move: 
https://github.com/facebook/rocksdb/wiki/Compaction-Trivial-Move

It reduces write amplification. It is disabled by default for universal and is enabled via the allow_trivial_move option: allow_trivial_move option https://github.com/facebook/rocksdb/blob/c66b4429ff4fb9d0791f515655b79c7551e9a1e2/include/rocksdb/universal_compaction.h#L75

When compaction is done from level N to level N+1, there is usually much work: 
- Read the input SSTs from level N
- Read the 10s input SSTs from level N+1, merge them and write 10s of SSTs to level N+1

The trivial move can be done when the key range for the input SST from level N does not overlap with the key range of any SST in level N+1. In this case the input SST fits in between the key ranged
of the level N+1 SST and rather than reading and re-writing SSTs, the trivial move optimization simply changes metadata (the MANIFEST) to indicate that the SST that had been on level N is now 
on level N+1, thus the name trivial move. There is no write amplification besides the rewrite of the MANIFEST. 


Code that checks whether is TrivialMove:
https://github.com/facebook/rocksdb/blob/a2b9be42b6d5ac4d44bcc6a9451a825440000769/db/compaction/compaction.cc#L291

A trivial move is not done when ...
- the compression (lz4, zstd, none) used for the input SST does not match the compression to be used for the output level. If bottommost_compression is zstd and non-bottom levels do not use zstd then trivial moves can be done up to Lmax-1 and compaction from Lmax-1 to Lmax cannot use trivial move (Lmax is max level, Lmax-1 is the next to max level). The logic for that is here.
- the SST moved to level N+1 would overlap too many SSTs in level N+2. The logic for that is here. And overlap too many SSTs means that the compaction from level N+1 to N+2 would exceed max_compaction_bytes. The max_compaction_bytes option is 0 by default, and when 0 is set to 25 * target_file_size_base. So overlap too many SSTs == overlap ~25 SSTs by default. This check is only for leveled compaction.

Two potential side effects of Trivial move are:

- Many small/uncompacted SST files
- Since, these files are not compacted, Compaction filters are not run on these files.


# Second trick: Use Ribbon Filter 
https://rocksdb.org/blog/2021/12/29/ribbon-filter.html

Since version 6.15 last year, RocksDB supports Ribbon filters, a new alternative to Bloom filters that save space, especially memory, at the cost of more CPU usage, mostly in constructing the filters in the background. Most applications with long-lived data (many hours or longer) will likely benefit from adopting a Ribbon+Bloom hybrid filter policy. Here we explain why and how.

